\section{Sensitivity to the background}
\label{app:background}
The value function is marginal with $B=50$ random training rows throughout.
Figure~\ref{fig:background} changes the background to $B=20$ or $B=200$ random rows or to $50$ $k$-means medoids (the training rows nearest the cluster centres, weighted by cluster size) for the \texttt{shap} Kernel explainer on German Credit ($10$ instances for the random forest, $20$ for boosting and the MLP) and ACS Employment ($30$), at the reference budget; each background has its own exact reference.
The exact top-3 set equals the one under $B=50$ for $0.23$--$0.47$ of the instances at $B=20$, $0.50$--$0.90$ at $B=200$ and $0.50$--$0.87$ for $k$-means, so the reference moves toward stability as $B$ grows without settling by $B=200$.
The paired difference of the single run's mean $P_c$ (the share of runs correct, averaged over instances, each run scored against the exact attribution of its own background) is at most $0.09$ in absolute value, with intervals that include zero except in two cells at the margin; the tolerance-aware differences are exactly zero.
These are subsets of the $40$ and $60$ instances.
Figure~\ref{fig:floorb} shows floor (b) as a function of the background: for random rows it rises with $B$ and lies below the run-to-run agreement of the \texttt{shap} Kernel explainer in every $d\ge16$ row, while against the paired Kernel estimator the sign is mixed; Figure~\ref{fig:floorbs} gives the $d\le10$ datasets at the largest sampled budget.

