\section{Robustness of the margin law}
\label{app:m5}
Figure~\ref{fig:m5} checks the budget law of Section~\ref{sec:budget} two ways.
With $\sigma$ the per-instance standard deviation of the run's attribution difference, estimated from the runs at the other budgets, the Gaussian argument predicts that the share of correct runs depends on $x=g\sqrt m/\sigma$ alone, as $\Phi(\beta x)$ with $\beta=1$ (panels a--c, d).
Antithetic permutation sampling collapses onto this curve ($\beta=0.9$--$1.05$ at $m_{\mathrm{eff}}\ge256$); the \texttt{shap} Kernel explainer collapses only at large budgets, and the paired Kernel estimator stays below $1$.
The interval-censored slope of $\log m^*$ on $\log g$ depends on the budget grid for the paired Kernel estimator ($-1.4$ to $-1.8$ on the full grid, $-1.2$ to $-1.7$ on every second budget, $-0.65$ to $-1.1$ on every third) and not for antithetic permutation (panel e).
The upper bound $20$ of a probit interval is a search bound reached when every point at large $x$ is correct, not an estimate.

